.docname {Output formats}
.include {docs}

The model makes one **building**. A **connector** writes it as bytes in one format. This page
covers the three formats, what each one holds, and how to add a fourth.

## One building, three files

.mermaid caption:{The worker hands the same result to every connector.}
    flowchart LR
        M[Model result<br/>building, cleaned dots, report] --> J[twin-json]
        M --> D[dxf]
        M --> P[ply]
        J --> W1[text frame or JSON file]
        D --> W2[text frame or DXF file]
        P --> W3[binary frame or PLY file]

| Id | Label | Holds | Sent as | File | Good for |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `twin-json` | Digital twin (JSON) | Walls, rooms, objects, report | text | `.json` | Other apps, simulations |
| `dxf` | Floor plan (DXF) | A 2D drawing of the same | text | `.dxf` | CAD programs |
| `ply` | Cleaned cloud (PLY) | The dots, after dropping strays | binary | `.ply` | 3D tools |

The worker writes **all three after every run**, and keeps all three. A viewer or a download asks
for one by its id.

## What a connector is

One small record, in `connectors/connector.hpp`:

| Field | Example | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `"twin-json"` | In `/ws/twin?format=<id>` and `/api/twin/<id>` |
| `label` | `"Digital twin (JSON)"` | The tab's name in the page |
| `mime` | `"application/json"` | The `Content-Type` of a download |
| `extension` | `".json"` | The name of a download: `twin-<seq>.json` |
| `binary` | `false` | Text or binary frame on the WebSocket |
| `write` | `to_json` | A function: the model's result in, bytes out |

The list of connectors is one function, `connectors()`, in `connector.cpp`. The server, the
worker and `/api/formats` all read that list. Nobody else names a format.

A connector may **throw**. Then that format is missing from that run; the others are kept (see
[Backend runtime](backend-runtime.qd)).

## The JSON twin

Covered in [Domain in code](domain-in-code.qd): the schema, a real file and every field.

- Keys come out in schema order, so a person can read the file.
- Numbers are rounded to millimetres. Angles are not.
- The writer **checks** the building first: ids unique, links pointing somewhere, outlines of 3
  points or more. A broken building throws rather than make a broken file.

## The DXF floor plan

DXF is a text format that CAD programs read. It is a list of pairs: a number on one line (a
**group code**: what the next line means), the value on the next.

```text
0          <- "a new entity starts"
LINE       <- it is a line
8          <- "layer"
WALLS      <- this layer
10         <- "start x"
-5.450
20         <- "start y"
-5.223
...
```

(From a real run; some lines are left out.)

| Layer | Drawn with | Of |
|---|---|---|
| `WALLS` | 4 lines per wall: both faces and both ends | Each wall, with its thickness |
| `ROOMS` | One closed polyline, and a text with the room's name (or its id) at the middle | Each room |
| `OBJECTS` | 4 lines per box, turned by `yaw` | Each object |

- Version **AC1015** (AutoCAD R2000), the first with polylines. Units: **metres**.
- Walls are drawn as an architect would, with both faces, not as one centre line.
- Object boxes are four **lines**, not a closed polyline: an importer that ignores layers would
  read every closed polyline as a room.
- Heights are left out: it is a plan.
- Numbers use a **dot** as decimal separator, on any computer's language setting.

**Example.** A wall from (0, 0) to (4, 0), thickness 0.10: the half thickness is 0.05, so the
faces are the lines (0, 0.05)–(4, 0.05) and (0, −0.05)–(4, −0.05), closed by two short ends: 4
lines in all. (Made-up numbers.)

## The PLY cloud

PLY is a simple format for point clouds. Here it is **binary**, little-endian.

```text
ply
format binary_little_endian 1.0
comment frame_id camera_init
element vertex 186427
property float x
property float y
property float z
end_header
<186427 × 12 bytes: x y z as float32>
```

- Only `x`, `y`, `z`. Brightness is left out: it is too noisy to show, and leaving it out keeps
  **12 bytes** per dot instead of 16.
- The file holds the **cleaned** dots (after dropping strays), in the robot's map frame.
- **Example, real.** The room of the [Overview](introduction.qd): 186,427 dots × 12 bytes =
  2,237,124 bytes, plus the header.

## Add a new format

| Step | Where | What |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `backend/src/connectors/<name>.cpp` and `include/.../<name>.hpp` | Write `std::string to_<name>(const ModelResult&)` |
| 2 | `backend/CMakeLists.txt` | Add the `.cpp` to `vcortex_core` |
| 3 | `connector.cpp` | Add one line to the list: id, label, type, extension, binary or text, the function |
| 4 | `frontend/src/components/renderers/` | Write a `<Name>Scene.vue`: payload in, 3D shapes out, and a `bounds` event |
| 5 | `frontend/src/components/TwinView.vue` | Add one `v-else-if` for the new id |

After steps 1–3 the format already shows up in `/api/formats`, streams on `/ws/twin` and
downloads from `/api/twin/<id>`; the page then lists it as a tab, and shows nothing until
steps 4–5 are done.

## Gaps found

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| A comment in `connectors/ply.cpp` calls the brightness "the first reading per cube"; the protocol header (`protocol/ingest.hpp`) says it is the cube's average | `connectors/ply.cpp`, `protocol/ingest.hpp` |
| The DXF writer has a TODO: no handles, and no `TABLES`, `BLOCKS` and `OBJECTS` sections. Open CAD programs and `ezdxf` read the file; AutoCAD may not | `connectors/dxf.cpp` |
| The format id `twin-json` is written in the frontend too (it is the first choice, and `TwinView.vue` tests for each id), so a new format needs a frontend change, not only a backend one | `HomeView.vue`, `TwinView.vue` |
